WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.120
The differences in our genes come about&nbsp;
through a natural process called mutation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.440
Mutation is extremely common. As a&nbsp;
cell copies its DNA before dividing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:21.120
it makes occasional typos. Most commonly,&nbsp;
a single base is substituted for another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.920
Sometimes a base is deleted or an extra base&nbsp;
is added. The cell is able to repair most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.680
these typos. But each round of cell division&nbsp;
introduces a few changes that are not repaired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.760
When DNA changes occur in cells that will give&nbsp;
rise to eggs or sperm, they can be passed down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.520
to offspring. Each one of us has about 60 new&nbsp;
variations that were not present in our parents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.600
People commonly use the terms mutant and mutation&nbsp;
to describe something undesirable or broken.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:58.160
But mutation is not always bad. Most DNA&nbsp;
changes fall in the large areas of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:04.320
genome that sit between genes and they usually&nbsp;
have no affect. When variations occur in areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.240
of the genome that code for proteins, they often&nbsp;
have consequences. These changes can effect the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.960
protein product itself or they can effect&nbsp;
when, where, or how much protein is made.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.880
Most of the time, mutation generates variations&nbsp;
that are neither good nor bad, just different.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.520
While a mutation is defined as any alteration&nbsp;
in the DNA sequence, biologists use the term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.480
“single nucleotide polymorphism” (SNP) to&nbsp;
refer to a single base pair alteration that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.640
is common in the population. Specifically, a&nbsp;
polymorphism is any genetic location at which at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.160
least two different sequences are found, with each&nbsp;
sequence present in at least 1% of the population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.480
Note that the term “polymorphism” is generally&nbsp;
used to refer to a normal variation, or one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:56.800
does not directly cause disease. Moreover, the&nbsp;
cutoff of at least 1% prevalence for a variation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.320
to be classified as a polymorphism is somewhat&nbsp;
arbitrary; if the frequency is lower than this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:08.480
the allele is typically regarded as a mutation.
SNPs are important as markers, or signposts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.640
for scientists to use when they look at&nbsp;
populations of organisms in an attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.880
find genetic changes that predispose individuals&nbsp;
to certain traits, including disease. On average,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:25.040
SNPs are found every 1,000–2,000 nucleotides in&nbsp;
the human genome, and scientists participating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:29.040
in the International HapMap Consortium&nbsp;
have mapped millions of these alterations.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.880
The DNA in any cell can be altered through&nbsp;
environmental exposure to certain chemicals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.000
ultraviolet radiation, other genetic insults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.960
or even errors that occur during&nbsp;
the process of replication.&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.920
Sickle-cell anemia is a disease caused by a change&nbsp;
in a single nucleotide, and it represents just one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.920
class of mutations called point mutations. The&nbsp;
alteration of a single nucleotide in the gene for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:59.360
the beta chain of the hemoglobin protein, the&nbsp;
oxygen-carrying protein that makes blood red,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:03.840
is all it takes to turn a normal hemoglobin&nbsp;
gene into a sickle-cell hemoglobin gene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:10.160
This single nucleotide change alters only one&nbsp;
amino acid in the protein chain, but the results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:15.520
are devastating. Molecules of sickle-cell&nbsp;
hemoglobin stick to one another, forming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:22.160
rigid rods. These rods cause a person's red blood&nbsp;
cells to take on a deformed, sickle-like shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:28.240
thus giving the disease its name. The rigid,&nbsp;
misshapen blood cells do not carry oxygen well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.280
and they also tend to clog capillaries, causing&nbsp;
an affected person's blood supply to be cut off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.880
to various tissues, including the brain and the&nbsp;
heart. Therefore, when an afflicted individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.640
exerts himself or herself even slightly, he or&nbsp;
she often experiences terrible pain, and he or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.440
she might even undergo heart attack or stroke&nbsp;
- all because of a single nucleotide mutation.&nbsp;

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.640
Mutations can also alter the way a gene&nbsp;
is read through either the insertion or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:01.120
the deletion of a single base. In these so-called&nbsp;
frameshift mutations, entire proteins are altered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:07.200
as a result of the deletion or insertion. This&nbsp;
occurs because nucleotides are read by ribosomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.960
in groups of three, called codons. Thus, if&nbsp;
the number of bases removed or inserted from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.080
a gene is not a multiple of three, the reading&nbsp;
frame for the rest of the protein is thrown off.&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.360
Changes in the DNA sequence can also&nbsp;
occur at the level of the chromosome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.840
in which large segments of&nbsp;
chromosomes are altered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:32.480
In this case, fragments of chromosomes can be&nbsp;
deleted, duplicated, inverted, translocated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:37.520
different chromosomes, or otherwise rearranged,&nbsp;
resulting in changes such as modification of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:43.600
gene dosage, the complete absence of genes,&nbsp;
or the alteration of gene sequence. The type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:49.120
of variation that occurs when entire areas of&nbsp;
chromosomes are duplicated or lost, called copy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:55.840
number variation (CNV), has especially important&nbsp;
implications for human disease and evolution.

